{
  "version": 3,
  "format": "story",
  "title": "Find the transaction holding your lock",
  "topic": "databases/postgres/lock-contention-pg-locks",
  "genre": "trace",
  "style": "blueprint",
  "series": "databases-hands-on-ops",
  "series_index": 3,
  "tags": [
    "databases",
    "postgres",
    "locks",
    "operations"
  ],
  "level": "intermediate",
  "cast": [
    {
      "id": "rookie",
      "name": "Mira",
      "role": "Curious junior engineer who states the plausible wrong assumption",
      "color": "#E4572E"
    },
    {
      "id": "senior",
      "name": "Dev",
      "role": "Calm staff engineer who asks for evidence and explains the mechanism",
      "color": "#2F6F8F"
    }
  ],
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "PID",
      "full": "Process Identifier",
      "note": "Identifier of a PostgreSQL backend process."
    },
    {
      "term": "pg_blocking_pids",
      "full": "Function listing sessions blocking a backend",
      "note": "Returns blockers for the supplied process identifier."
    },
    {
      "term": "pg_locks",
      "full": "PostgreSQL view of outstanding locks",
      "note": "A row-level wait often appears as a wait for the holder transaction identifier."
    }
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Mira opens a second database session. The account update freezes; Dev points at the first terminal.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "db.table",
          "id": "accounts",
          "name": "accounts (illustrative rows)",
          "columns": [
            "id",
            "balance"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              50
            ],
            [
              2,
              80
            ],
            [
              3,
              25
            ]
          ],
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "Session A leaves its update open. Session B updates the same row and waits.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "seq.diagram",
          "id": "sessions",
          "actors": [
            "Session A",
            "Row",
            "Session B",
            "Observer"
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "from": "Session A",
              "to": "Row",
              "label": "update"
            }
          ],
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "seq.diagram",
          "id": "sessions",
          "messages": [
            {
              "from": "Session B",
              "to": "Row",
              "label": "update waits"
            }
          ],
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "Session B waits"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "PID means Process Identifier. Find the waiting session in pg_stat_activity, then ask pg_blocking_pids which sessions block it.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "define",
          "term": "PID",
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "code.editor",
          "id": "waiter",
          "title": "Find waiting backend",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, wait_event_type",
            "FROM pg_stat_activity;"
          ],
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "So the blocker function gives me a list, instead of making me accuse whichever query looks slowest?",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "code.editor",
          "id": "blockers",
          "title": "Ask PostgreSQL",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid)",
            "FROM pg_stat_activity",
            "WHERE wait_event_type = 'Lock';"
          ],
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "define",
          "term": "pg_blocking_pids",
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "pg_blocking_pids: blocker list"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "Exactly. Inspect each blocker’s state and transaction start. Idle in transaction can still hold locks.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "code.editor",
          "id": "inspect",
          "title": "Inspect blocker",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pid, state, xact_start",
            "FROM pg_stat_activity",
            "WHERE pid IN (",
            " SELECT unnest(pg_blocking_pids(pid))",
            " FROM pg_stat_activity",
            " WHERE wait_event_type = 'Lock');"
          ],
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "Would pg_locks always show a row lock directly on the accounts row?",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "Mira checks the lock view",
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "Often not. Row locks are stored on disk; a waiter commonly waits on the holder transaction ID in pg_locks.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "define",
          "term": "pg_locks",
          "at": "start"
        },
        {
          "do": "code.show",
          "id": "lock-fields",
          "lang": "sql",
          "title": "Useful lock fields",
          "code": [
            "SELECT pid, locktype, transactionid, granted",
            "FROM pg_locks;"
          ],
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "So I should not match only on relation and guess which session owns the wait.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "Mira removes relation-only guess",
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "senior",
      "text": "Right. Use the blocker function, inspect granted state and the owning transaction, then verify who is waiting.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "waiter → blocker → owning transaction",
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "speaker": "rookie",
      "text": "My forgotten transaction! I will roll it back in Session A, confirm B resumes, and fix the application’s transaction scope.",
      "visuals": [
        {
          "do": "layout",
          "template": "board-full"
        },
        {
          "do": "seq.diagram",
          "id": "sessions",
          "messages": [
            {
              "from": "Session A",
              "to": "Row",
              "label": "ROLLBACK"
            },
            {
              "from": "Row",
              "to": "Session B",
              "label": "released; B resumes",
              "reply": true
            }
          ],
          "at": "start",
          "actors": [
            "Session A",
            "Row",
            "Session B"
          ]
        },
        {
          "do": "sketch.note",
          "text": "verify Session B resumes",
          "at": "start"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-info.html",
    "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/view-pg-locks.html",
    "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"
  ],
  "key_points": [
    "Use pg_blocking_pids to identify sessions blocking a waiting backend.",
    "Inspect blocker state and transaction age; an idle transaction can still hold locks.",
    "A row-level wait often appears as a transaction ID wait in pg_locks; confirm that the owning transaction ends."
  ],
  "expect": [
    {
      "beat": 0,
